  prompt speed: `-b 2048 -ub 2048` is the sized safe batch for this card (buffer-fit rule, prereg #23) - a bigger ubatch batches host-resident weights across PCIe, but the measured numbers behind this lever (+73%, 199.9 -> 345.9 pp2048, prereg #19; 360.76 at pp4096/ub4096, L-26) are from the CPU-expert MoE placement (-ot exps=CPU) and are NOT a prediction for this row - the prefill law that licenses them is scoped to that tier (L-26; the dense control violates its form). Known boundary: fully in VRAM the same flag LOSES 39% (prereg #19 P-2), so the batch is sized against VRAM headroom and capped at 2048 outside the measured tier.

  dense-SPLIT speculation (measured, prereg #69): a small same-family draft is the best speculation cell on this box - `-md draft.gguf -ngld 0 --spec-draft-n-max 2` bought **+33%** decode on a 14B split target (5.5 -> 7.4 tok/s, 76% acceptance, novel code), and the draft costs ZERO VRAM because it runs on CPU. Mechanism: the K+1-token verify batch reads each CPU-resident layer once, so the CPU share of every token amortizes. Keep K=2: every measured K>=3 landed AT OR BELOW no-draft baseline (the CPU draft spends the same RAM bandwidth the amortization saves), so llama.cpp's default 3 already loses here.

  speculation: if you write CODE, add `--spec-type ngram-simple --spec-ngram-simple-size-m 12 --spec-ngram-simple-size-n 4` - one flag family, no download, identical output. THE DRAFT LENGTH IS THE LEVER (X-1, staked and measured): on pre-Ampere cards the verify pass switches kernels at width 9, so drafts of 4-7 are strictly dominated - measured 48-51 tok/s at m=4-7 vs **88.5 at m=8 and 124-132 at m=12-24**, against a 22.8 baseline: **up to 5.8x single-stream** on copy-heavy work, byte-identical output. Never draft 4-7 on this card class: m>=8 or leave speculation off. On Ampere+ the boundary is unverified (`bench --contribute` settles it). Prose gains nothing at any m (1.01x): the drafter copies spans from your context, and prose has none.
  (upper bounds from this row's own decomposition, at 100% acceptance: 3.00x for a DRAFT
  MODEL at K=2 - it verifies K+1 tokens per weight read - and 2.10x for the ngram drafter
  above, which measured ~2.1 accepted tokens per verify round. A verify batch amortizes weight
  READS only; measured gains are below both and MoE offload pays a union tax on top.)
